## Task topology and kernel support

Lower numeric values have higher priority:

- priority 0: TC2 bootstrap only; it creates the complete task topology and
  exits before normal service scheduling;
- priority 1: name server and clock notifier;
- priority 2: clock server and bounded CAN RX notifier;
- priority 3: CAN server, CAN TX notifier, and independent dispatch safety
  supervisor;
- priority 4: terminal, reservation, sensor, and dispatch servers plus the
  terminal RX/TX notifiers, sensor courier, dispatch ticker, and interactive
  command/UI task, with round-robin progress inside the control-plane class;
- priority 15: silent idle task.

The TC2 bootstrap must outrank the services it creates.  Under this kernel's
strict fixed-priority scheduler, the previous priority-8 bootstrap could be
starved by the aggregate priority-1..4 clock/CAN/sensor workload before it
created the dispatcher and UI.  TC1 and the other build modes retain their
original bootstrap priority.

Before entering C, the boot path explicitly zeroes the linker-defined BSS
range.  Raw network-boot images do not carry the NOLOAD BSS, and the loader
may reuse RAM from an earlier image; without this step, stale CAN flags and
timer/UART waiter pointers can corrupt the new scheduler.  Kernel startup also
resets its event-list roots as defense in depth.

The reservation, sensor, and dispatch servers are the single writers for their
respective state.  The sensor courier is the only CAN sensor consumer.  The
dispatcher waits for a bounded sensor-server/courier READY handshake before
publishing a healthy scheduler; a missing handshake leaves launch disabled.
Within one dispatcher pass, an equal-priority courier may publish heartbeat
`N+1` after the dispatcher cached time `N`; Stage 8 treats only that bounded
one-tick future skew as fresh.  A larger future skew, a heartbeat older than
the normal age limit, or any new courier receive/time failure still fails
closed and is reported separately from reservation and sensor-sequence
failures.
The dispatch ticker uses the raw hardware timer rather than depending on
`Delay()`, and an independent supervisor watches dispatch heartbeat,
logical-clock progress, and real microsecond elapsed time.  If any of them
stalls while a train may be moving, the supervisor queues priority speed-zero
commands for every active train.

TC2's kernel-facing changes are cooperative terminal RX/TX notifiers and
waiter queues, bounded CAN RX service, queued CAN TX completion, IPC peer-exit
cleanup, and a silent idle path.  When a server exits, queued `Send` callers
and delivered callers awaiting `Reply` are unblocked with an error before its
task descriptor can be reused.  None of the safety hot paths prints directly
to a possibly full UART.

The controller has no floating-point requirement.  All AArch64 target code is
compiled with `-mgeneral-regs-only`; link verification rejects any instruction
whose operands name SIMD/FP registers.  Every exception frame therefore needs
only `x0`-`x30`, `ELR`, and `SPSR`.  `context_frame_layout.h` is shared by C
and assembly, and compile-time plus host regressions prevent layout or build
policy drift.

## Launch and destination map

The six horizontal launch bays are:

```text
A=EN5  B=EN4  C=EN7  D=EN10  E=EN9  F=EN3
```

The two centre vertical buffer tracks are not launch bays.  The eight
operator-facing destination regions have two directed localization-anchor
sides:

```text
d1=A12/A15  d2=A4/B15  d3=C11/B6  d4=C10/B2
d5=E5/D5    d6=E13/D14 d7=D7/D8   d8=C15/D11
```

`tc2_track_model.c` is the single source of truth for these 6 starts and
8x2 directed endpoint sides.  Boot-time validation checks all entries against
the generated 144-node Track B graph used for physical Track D: exactly
80 direction-specific sensor nodes (40 physical reverse pairs), 22
branch/merge turnout pairs, all six entry nodes, and all 16 destination
anchors must agree.  Parser, route planner, diagnostics, prediction, and the
Track D operations UI consume this shared model instead of maintaining
independent name tables.

These names bound physical parts intervals containing the operator marks;
they are not aliases for the stopping points.  D1 splits the CAN-side
A12-to-A15 24064 interval at 407/407 mm.  D2, D3, D4, and D8 split their
identified 24077 intervals at 219/218, 176/175, 180/179, and 202/202 mm.
D5 and D6 split their 24664 intervals at 188/188 and 141/141 mm.  Capture
`7.pcapng` rejected the former d1=A12/A11 mapping: T14/A/80/d1 observed
A1,C13,E7,D7,D9,E12,C8, no A11/A12, and stopped about 330 mm short.
The measured A-to-D7 direction still uses a fixed physical base of 280 mm
after sensor D7.
Supervised T14/A/d7 calibration additionally applies a bounded
signed speed-dependent endpoint-control correction.  The current correction
anchors at speeds 20/40/60/80/100/120 are respectively
+66/+50/+32/+15/-99/-555 mm.  Stage 32 increased the C13-to-E7 graph edge by
exactly 95 mm and rebased every controller correction by -95 mm; the fixed
+280 mm operator base and every effective physical endpoint are therefore
unchanged.  Every integer speed 1..120 is covered by piecewise interpolation
in provisional-velocity space; speeds below 20 clamp to +66 mm.  Positive
values delay braking and negative values request braking earlier.  At speeds
whose effective control point lies before physical
sensor D7, the planner preserves the same scalar endpoint but uses E7 as the
localization anchor; the user-visible destination remains D7.  These are
explicit single-run physical anchors, not yet accepted repeated calibration.
The measured D7-facing side is `CONFIRMED`; the remaining 15 directed sides
are explicitly `PROVISIONAL` until individual measurements replace the
parts-derived values.  Geometry evidence is kept
separate from train/speed motion calibration: a confirmed endpoint position
does not imply a calibrated braking curve for every train or speed.
`trackview` and `trips` label the
selected `anchor_sensor`, base offset, speed correction, effective endpoint
offset, and whether that directed base is `CONFIRMED` or `PROVISIONAL`.

Stage 19 adds a pure route-relative prediction planner shared by every
`A-F`/`d1-d8`/directed-side/speed combination.  Given a validated route to
one catalog anchor, it derives four separate scalar positions:

```text
physical operator endpoint
speed-corrected control endpoint
speed-zero command point
minimum conservative reservation ceiling
```

Each scalar is re-anchored to the latest direction-specific node at or before
that point, so a negative high-speed correction can move the command point to
an earlier sensor without changing the logical destination.  Arithmetic is
signed and range checked; a command point before the route start becomes an
explicit immediate-stop plan rather than wrapping.  The exact evidence label
is restricted to T14/A/d7/side 0 without a reversal. All other plans are
labelled `TRANSFERRED_T14_A_D7`, including other trains, origins, destination
sides, and reversed routes.  The transferred point model never shrinks the
independent conservative collision reservation.

Host regression exhaustively covers all
`6 * 8 * 2 * 120 = 11,520` start/destination-side/speed plans on the generated
Track D graph, plus route mismatch, range, re-anchoring, evidence, and safety
ceiling invariants.

Stage 20 binds this prediction metadata to the route actually selected for an
ordinary fixed-start `dispatch`.  It recomputes evidence from the selected
route, downgrades a nominal exact profile when the route is noncanonical, and
never shrinks the independently computed physical reservation.  The Stage 20
integration is diagnostic metadata only: it deliberately does not change
