The dispatcher evaluates both sides; the first name is not a hard-coded
default.  Unavailable-aware Dijkstra produces the minimum graph-cost forward
and reversal-enabled candidate for each side, using physical distance plus a
direction-change penalty.  After braking, turnout, body-clearance, and carried
hold validation, the dispatcher selects the lowest-cost surviving candidate;
an exact forward/reversal cost tie keeps the forward candidate.  For a stopped
`CURRENT` train it also evaluates an explicit leading-reversal alternative so
an unsafe under-train turnout change is not mistaken for a zero-distance
arrival.  This is candidate-shortest planning, not a claim that the code
enumerates every k-shortest graph alternative after a candidate fails a
post-search safety check.

