## Stage 26 atomic dispatcher projection publication

The dispatcher now exposes the Stage-25 projection through a compact,
fixed-width, read-only IPC contract.  It first builds and validates the
complete projection in private scratch.  Reservation CAS still decides
ownership; a conflict or failed CAS publishes nothing.  Only after CAS
success does the dispatcher copy an immutable header and waypoint payload,
then commit the publication valid bit and the job summary token.

Consumers read a header followed by pages of at most eight waypoints.  Every
page request must repeat the exact train, publication serial, reservation
generation, and launch epoch from that header.  Any old or mixed token is
returned as `STALE`; an unsupported trip is `NOT_READY`; an out-of-range
page is `BOUNDS`.  Unused page entries are canonical zeroes.  Re-stage,
cancel, control failure, and physical removal invalidate the UI publication
immediately, so no display can continue animating an obsolete route.
Invalidating the display copy does not release physical protection:
reservation ownership remains until the existing cancel/STOP_HOLD/operator
remove workflow releases it.  An arrived job retains its projection until
cancel or remove.

This publication contract applies to ordinary fixed A-F dispatches whose
prediction and physical endpoint are authoritative.  `CURRENT`,
localization-only, and provisional `caldispatch` trips deliberately return
`NOT_READY`; the dispatcher does not invent their origin, direction, or
geometry.  The host regression reconstructs every published page, checks
token staleness and lifecycle invalidation, and retains the Stage-25
11,520-case geometry evidence as its upstream route basis.  Stage 26 still
does not advance simulated time, move a train, synthesize live sensors, or
claim a new physical Track D run.

## Stage 27 deterministic offline multi-train runtime

The immutable Stage-26 publication can now be consumed by an allocation-free,
prediction-only runtime with 16 fixed train slots.  It advances each accepted
projection with the shared calibrated fixed-point velocity model, supports
`uint32_t` tick wrap, interpolates terminal-cell coordinates without floats,
and emits start, wait, resume, directed-sensor pulse, turnout, reversal,
arrival, and failure events.  Every event is explicitly marked
`PREDICTED_OFFLINE` and `is_live=0`; this module never sends CAN traffic,
changes a turnout, or acquires/releases the physical reservation service.

Conflict arbitration is deterministic and fail-closed.  A train claims the
current movement window by graph vertex, normalized physical edge,
co-located direction-sensor detector pair, turnout, and destination
footprint.  An overlapping candidate remains stopped in `WAIT_CONFLICT`.
Older waiting work wins before a younger launch, with train number as the
final stable tie break.  Claims shrink as progress advances and an arrived
train retains only its destination footprint.  Projection ingestion is
atomic: incomplete, reordered, stale-token, non-canonical-tail, aliased, or
out-of-bounds pages cannot create a runnable trip.

The sanitizer regression covers all `6 * 8 * 120 = 5,760` A-F/d1-d8/speed
requests, 1/2/3/4/6/16-train cardinalities, all 80 direction-specific
sensors (including separate A11/A12 identities), FIFO conflict release,
event provenance, tick wrap, and paged-publication failures.  This is still
offline software evidence.  The periodic command loop, live renderer
connection, and any new physical Track D claim remain separate later stages.

## Stage 28 pure offline route planner

`tc2_offline_planner.c` is a side-effect-free value transformer for every
A-F origin, d1-d8 operator destination, and integer speed 1..120.  It uses
the validated Track D catalog, the same generalized-cost shortest-path
machinery and reversal rules as the dispatcher, the shared calibrated
prediction model, and the Stage-25 projection contract.  Its output is one
complete immutable projection that the offline runtime can consume; it
neither grants motion authority nor calls CAN, IPC, live sensors, or the
physical reservation service.

Planning is deterministic and fail-closed.  Rebuilding the same request must
produce the same route, prediction, publication token, and waypoint bytes.
Invalid arguments, a mutated catalog, an unreachable graph, a route-guard
failure, or a projection mismatch return an empty non-runnable result.
The input track graph is checked byte-for-byte after the matrix so the
planner cannot hide state in shared topology.

The ASan/UBSan regression builds and validates all
`6 * 8 * 120 = 5,760` requests twice, exercises a synthetic edge cut that
requires a sensor-pair reversal, proves a cut origin is unreachable, rejects
catalog mutation and invalid bounds, and statically excludes CAN, IPC,
reservation, and live-sensor calls.  These results establish deterministic
offline planning only.  Periodic command processing and terminal animation
are integrated by Stages 29-31 below; physical Track D stopping remains
separate acceptance work.

## Stage 29 nonblocking atomic terminal frames

The incremental renderer now publishes through a bounded three-slot transmit
pump: one immutable active frame, one pending frame, and one private staging
frame.  Each slot is 16 KiB and every drain attempt is an all-or-none chunk of
at most 192 bytes.  `TerminalTryWrite` either accepts the complete chunk,
returns `WOULD_BLOCK` without accepting a byte, or reports a hard error.
Backpressure therefore preserves the exact byte offset and cannot interleave
two frame generations.  A hard or ambiguous output failure discards queued
bytes, invalidates deltas, and requires a later full redraw.

Begin/stage/commit is one publication transaction.  Generations are nonzero
wrapping serial numbers; equal, older, and exactly half-range values are
rejected.  The caller's complete style reset, cursor position, prompt, and
partially typed input are appended verbatim to the frame.  The pump does not
invent prompt bytes.  Conflicting delta publication is rejected until a full
frame re-establishes terminal state.  Host tests cover partial draining,
repeated backpressure, pending-frame replacement rules, generation wrap,
oversize and malformed transactions, output failure, full-redraw recovery,
and exact prompt restoration.  This transport evidence is not CAN traffic or
physical train motion.

## Stage 30 bounded offline controller

`tc2_offline_controller.c` owns the complete prediction-only pipeline in
static/BSS storage:

```text
planner -> deterministic runtime -> provenance overlay
        -> delta renderer -> atomic nonblocking transmit pump
```

The controller supports at most 16 records, validates every A-F,
d1-d8, and speed-1..120 request before staging it, starts one or all staged
trips, advances only on caller-supplied absolute 10 ms ticks, and removes a
trip without leaving an old-generation marker.  Runtime events are accepted
only when their train, plan generation, source, and non-live prediction
provenance match the retained job.  A mismatch or impossible transition
fails the controller closed until an explicit reset.  Rendering and draining
are independently bounded so terminal backpressure never changes simulated
time or conflict ordering.

The retained overlay gives every active train a stable distinct color, pulses
its marker on refresh, and flashes the exact direction-specific detector
label for 300 ms.  Co-located directions such as A11/A12 remain independent
events.  Conflicting trains enter deterministic `WAIT_CONFLICT`; disjoint
trains advance together.  Every controller frame contains the exact evidence
label:

```text
OFFLINE SIMULATION / PREDICTED / NO CAN / NO PHYSICAL MOTION
```

That label is a safety boundary, not decoration.  No controller API sends
locomotive speed, changes a turnout, reads a live detector, or acquires a
physical reservation.

