cases.  It also checks graph adjacency and distance sums, turnout sequences,
direction-specific sensor replay, prediction bounds, and reservation guards.
These results are deterministic offline software evidence.  They do not
establish CAN delivery, turnout actuation, physical train motion, live
detector timing, endpoint accuracy, or real collision avoidance.

## Normal workflow

Before the first trip, physically place each stopped train at its declared
A–F bumper, facing into the layout.

Stage any number of jobs up to the 16-record scheduler capacity.  The same
launch epoch may use each physical A–F bay at most once:

```text
dispatch 77 A 8 d5
dispatch 78 C 8 d2
dispatch 79 F 7 d4
go
```

Every dispatch command contains the train number, origin, speed, and
destination.  `go` submits all currently staged jobs as one epoch.  The
scheduler makes one oldest-first admission pass.  Each candidate is planned
from fresh sequential reservation and sensor snapshots, and the reservation
server performs the atomic conflict check after planning; the resulting
admitted set is maximal for that pass.  Jobs that conflict remain stopped in
`WAIT_ROUTE` and retry in oldest-first order.  Admitted jobs enter `PREPARING`,
queue their turnout commands without blocking the dispatcher, wait for every
matching CS3 response, and cross one common settle barrier before one queued
speed-command wave.  The CAN protocol still serializes those commands and
their confirmations; the UI records the resulting launch skew rather than
claiming physically simultaneous transmission.  A later disjoint job can be
prepared while other trains remain in motion; this is not limited to two
trains.  Temporary CAN queue pressure is backpressure, not a transport
failure: a job remains `PREPARING` or `READY` and retries without bypassing
its confirmation barrier.

The command parser accepts the Märklin user-speed range `1..120`, but an
operational TC2 run must use only train/speed/direction combinations that have
been measured on Track D and shown to move without stalling.  An uncalibrated
low value is not a valid graded or unattended operating speed merely because
the parser accepts it.  A model-derived braking deadline is never treated as
physical sensor evidence.  For a virtual endpoint, the directed anchor must
first be observed in route order; the train then continues through the
configured offset and stops by the timer/model.  This arrival remains
explicitly estimated, and the complete endpoint/braking hold stays reserved
until the normal `cancel` then `remove` operator workflow.  A stop before its
required anchor is `STOP_UNCONFIRMED`; a downstream guard sensor observed
before the model stop fails closed rather than silently accepting an
overshoot.

## Supervised all-speed prediction workflow

To avoid waiting for a complete per-speed calibration table before collecting
useful runs, Stage 15 retains a deliberately isolated first-pass predictor:

```text
caldispatch 14 A <speed 1..120> d7
go
trips
```

This command accepts exactly train 14, launch bay A, destination d7, and one
integer speed from 1 through 120.  It rejects `CURRENT`, every other
train/origin/destination, reversal or D8-side route, and any run while another
managed job exists.  Once it is staged, ordinary jobs are rejected until the
calibration job completes the normal cancel/remove lifecycle.  Tests must be
supervised and should progress upward from a known moving speed; parser
acceptance does not prove that a very low command can overcome physical
stiction.

Before the first ordered sensor interval, the provisional velocity curve
continuously interpolates every speed 1..120 using the shape of the old seed
knots, normalized to the observed steady T14/A-direction speed-20 value:

```text
speed 20 = 396 um per 10 ms tick = 39.6 mm/s
```

The first stopping prediction uses a constant-deceleration scaling from the
same run:

```text
stop_um(speed) =
  ceil(10243 * velocity_um_per_tick(speed)^2 / 396^2)
```

Thus speed 20 predicts exactly 10,243 micrometres from the speed-zero request
to the pickup-leading-edge stopping point, while speed 120 initially predicts
261,275 micrometres.  The UI also prints a whole-millimetre rounded display
(11 mm and 262 mm respectively), but that rounded-up display value is never
used for point-deadline arithmetic.  After every accepted ordered sensor
interval, Stage 15 replaces that fallback with:

```text
observed_velocity_um_per_tick =
  round(segment_distance_mm * 1000 / elapsed_10ms_ticks)
```

The velocity-squared stopping prior is recomputed from that observation.
After every accepted sensor event, the timer is re-anchored and speed zero is
scheduled at:

```text
anchor_tick +
ceil((remaining_to_user_D7_mm * 1000 - predicted_stop_um) /
     velocity_um_per_tick)
```

For the confirmed A-to-D7 direction, physical sensor D7 is 280 mm before the
operator endpoint.  The supervised controller applies these signed
speed-correction anchors:

```text
speed:                  20   40   60   80   100   120
correction after base:  +66  +50  +32  +15  -99  -555 mm
effective D7 offset:    346  330  312  295  181  -275 mm
```

The effective A-to-user-D7 distances are therefore
2570/2554/2536/2519/2405/1949 mm at those speeds.  All intervening integer
speeds interpolate continuously in provisional-velocity space; speeds below
20 clamp to +66 mm.  The graph route is now 95 mm longer while each
correction is 95 mm smaller, which is why these physical distances are
unchanged.  A valid observed interval re-estimates velocity and
re-anchors the deadline at runtime.  Negative correction is a control-timing
adjustment, not negative track geometry.  At speed 120 the request point is
275 mm before physical sensor D7, equivalently 101 mm after E7; E7 is therefore
used as the localization anchor while the logical destination remains D7.
The collision reservation still covers the complete conservative braking and
turnout footprint.  The old Stage-9 rule requested zero
immediately at physical D7, while the Stage-10 +180 mm run still stopped
100 mm before the user endpoint.

This point timer does not replace collision protection.  Reservations and
turnout guards still use the much larger shared
`Tc2MotionBrakingDistanceMm()` envelope, the physical D7 anchor is required
before timer arrival, the sensor journal remains active while coasting, and a
stop without that anchor evidence becomes `STOP_UNCONFIRMED`.  Reaching a
later route sensor before the virtual endpoint stop is an overshoot/fault and
causes a fail-closed stop.
`trips` identifies the run as `PROVISIONAL_T14_A_D7` and prints velocity,
exact predicted stopping distance plus its rounded display value, the last
sensor/tick/distance anchor, predicted speed-zero request tick, actual request
tick, matching CAN-confirmation tick, signed request timing error, and the
actual stop trigger.  `cal` prints representative knots and states that all
intermediate integer speeds are interpolated.  If the exact no-reversal
A-to-D7 profile is unavailable, `caldispatch` remains stopped in `WAIT_ROUTE`;
it never launches an unmodelled alternate.

After a train has settled at a destination, a new trip can be staged from its
managed position:

```text
dispatch 77 CURRENT 8 d3
go
```

`CURRENT` is accepted only when the dispatcher has a retained position and
live reservation for that train.  It is not an operator-supplied guess.  The
old hold is copied from the reservation server and atomically unioned with the
new route under an expected old destination and generation; a stale or
conflicting replacement leaves the old hold unchanged.

Even an exactly corroborated arrival keeps its old body/tail hold until
ordered progress on the new trip makes it safe to release.  Every `CURRENT`
plan preserves the preceding CS3-confirmed final-leg turnout plan.  A setting
for a turnout inside the carried hold may be reused only when it is identical,
and its command is deliberately omitted so the turnout is not actuated under
the train.  A required leading direction change is issued and confirmed while
the train is stopped, before it can join the common launch barrier.

If the arrival position is estimated, the dispatcher uses only a
same-direction forward candidate and strictly forbids any leave-then-reenter
of the carried physical footprint before localization.  The initial speed is
no faster than the smaller of the old and requested speeds.  A first plausible
expected sensor does not release the old footprint because it could be the one
false report.  Only a second later, physically plausible route occurrence
localizes the train, releases the carried ambiguity through the normal
body-tail window, and permits the requested speed.

If an exact or estimated `CURRENT` route would require an unsafe carried-hold
turnout change, the train first follows the already confirmed direction to a
two-sensor clearance/localization point, stops, and automatically restages the
original destination.  This repeats conservatively, with a hard 16-phase
bound, until the requested route is safe; the operator does not issue another
command.  A foreign reservation, blocked destination, exhausted phase bound,
or absence of both a requested and a safe clearance route leaves the train
stopped in `WAIT_ROUTE`.

Inspection and lifecycle commands are:

```text
trips
trackview
sensors
positions
cancel <train>
remove <train>
```

`cancel` sends speed zero and retains protection while the motion model waits
through a speed-derived conservative settling interval, measured from the
fresh tick after the matching CAN confirmation.  `remove` is accepted only
after that settled stop and means the operator has physically lifted the
train; only then is its remaining reservation released.

## Unavailable track

An operator may remove a physical landmark and its reverse direction from
route finding:

```text
block <node>
unblock <node>
blocks
```

Blocking is rejected while the physical node is active or reserved.  A
waiting job replans around blocked nodes and reservations; the reservation
server performs the final atomic conflict check so a stale planning sample
cannot launch two trains into the same footprint.

## Routing and direction changes

The automatic planner:

- compares both directed sides of every d1–d8 region;
- excludes the physical pair for every blocked, occupied, or other-train
  reserved node;
- permits reversal transitions only between a sensor and its reverse sensor;
- divides a reversal route into motion legs;
- configures only the current leg's turnouts;
- performs a zero-distance leading reversal while stopped and before any
  positive-speed launch command;
- rejects consecutive zero-distance reversals as a non-canonical route;
- for any zero-motion result, confirms the selected final braking-extension
  turnout plan before publishing `ARRIVED`; a leading reversal must also
  finish its post-confirmation settle interval;
- preconfigures the directed braking extension beyond a final target so a
  possible overrun cannot enter an unmonitored turnout branch;
- stops and settles before issuing reverse, queues the next leg's turnout
  commands while stopped, waits for every CS3 confirmation, settles again,
  and then resumes;
- rejects a reversal whose next required turnout is closer than one train
  body plus model uncertainty, and applies the same protection to a
  `CURRENT` departure;
- if the reversal-shaped sensor report is the one false report, retains the
  whole incoming ambiguity corridor and full-speed forward stopping guard,
  and forbids every next-leg turnout resource in that area from changing;
- at a reversal, sends speed zero when the fast-bound approach reaches the
  calibrated stopping-distance trigger; the larger reserved braking guard
  contains the uncertainty/overrun envelope, sensors remain live during
  coast, and missing reversal evidence fails closed after the speed-derived
  settle interval;
- never applies a future-leg turnout plan across a reversal boundary.

Physical distance is recomputed from ordered route occurrences.  Repeated
nodes are not confused with an earlier traversal, and a reversal hop
contributes no track distance.

## Collision avoidance and reservations

The reservation server is the single writer for physical ownership.  Each
route claim includes both directed nodes for every physical landmark plus:

- the train body/tail behind its confirmed position;
- the complete remaining route;
- conservative braking guards at the final destination and every reversal;
- motion-model uncertainty.

Updates are atomic and carry a per-train plan generation.  A `CURRENT` plan
replacement also compares the expected old destination and generation before
changing any owner.  A sensor event or footprint update from an older
generation cannot advance the train.  As
ordered sensor progress is confirmed, the train releases track behind its
tail while retaining every future motion and stopping guard.  Arrival keeps a
conservative hold zone covering the train body and observed braking envelope.
An exact target report awaiting later corroboration instead retains its
anchor-based remaining footprint, and a later `CURRENT` trip keeps the union
until two-evidence localization.  A timer-only stop whose last confirmed
occurrence is still before the target is not an arrival: it remains in
`STOP_UNCONFIRMED` with the complete remaining hold until operator removal.

Turnout commands are sent only after the protected footprint is owned.
Unexpected turnout activity between `CURRENT` staging and its reservation CAS
is compared against the preserved ARRIVED baseline and rejected before the old
plan changes.  The two central double-turnout pairs are reserved and
commanded as interlocked physical resources; a curved command first places
its mate straight.  A
second train cannot reserve the physical turnout while the first train or its
tail may occupy it.  The CAN server accepts turnout batches only from the
registered dispatcher, while an unexpected physical turnout report during
motion fails the active trip closed.  The TC2 build disables legacy `tr`,
`sw`, `route`, `stop`, `demo`, `reserve`, and `release` commands even when the
scheduler is idle; all movement and protection changes go through
`dispatch`/`go`/`cancel`/`remove`.

## Sensor attribution and one-error behavior

The sensor courier is the only CAN sensor consumer.  It processes at most four
raw CAN frames per burst before blocking on the hardware timer, so continuous
status or non-sensor traffic cannot monopolize its priority class.  The
priority-0 bootstrap creates the complete topology before service load begins,
and the sensor courier, dispatcher ticker, and UI share the priority-4
round-robin class with the servers they use.  Continuous CAN traffic, long UI
output, or terminal backpressure therefore cannot suppress bootstrap
completion or the 20 ms dispatcher tick.  The bounded CAN RX hardware
notifier remains priority 2 and the independent emergency supervisor remains
priority 3.
Rising events are attributed through the reservation server and stored in a
bounded per-train journal with reservation generation and a monotonic sequence.

Launch and continued motion also require the sensor server to be registered
and its courier to have been created and to report ready.  CAN receive or
forwarding failures and sensor-clock failures are exposed in the snapshot;
any new failure after route preparation prevents launch or stops the affected
motion.  Failure paths and every bounded successful receive burst block on the
hardware timer.  Startup-stage serial diagnostics identify state
initialization, registration, reservation lookup, and courier creation before
the normal UI begins.

For each motion leg the route monitor expects ordered sensor occurrences:

- the expected sensor advances normally;
- the next-next sensor may advance once while recording one missing sensor;
- duplicate/old sequence numbers never advance;
- one unrelated attributed sensor is ignored and counted;
- one globally unattributed rising report is ignored for the active set and
  counted, while a second fails every affected trip closed;
- a second missing/spurious error, journal loss, generation mismatch, sensor
  service failure, or attribution failure stops the train and retains its
  reservation.

A target-shaped pulse cannot release track unless a later ordered occurrence
corroborates it.  Without that corroboration, speed zero is still sent, the
anchor-based remaining footprint is retained, and the position is marked
estimated.  A later `CURRENT` command is safe because it carries that entire
owner set into an expected-plan atomic replacement, forbids ambiguous
turnout/reversal choices, and requires two later ordered occurrences before
shrinking it.  If direct routing is not yet safe, the bounded internal
clearance/localization phase stops and automatically replans toward the
original d1–d8 request. A sensor event or unsolicited turnout change between
staging and preparation, or a turnout change while the train is held at
`ARRIVED`, prevents launch instead of being absorbed into a new baseline.  If
the final target report is the one missing sensor, the same conservative rule
applies.

The course protocol marks the 16-bit sensor timestamp as ignorable, so CAN
arrival order is authoritative.  A redundant explicit `1 -> 1` report is
suppressed while the sensor is already high.  A later delivered protocol
`0 -> 1` is instead accepted as a new occupation because it may be the first
evidence after a lost release; identical opaque timestamp bytes cannot prove
that it is a retransmission.  This policy accepts long gaps and timestamp wrap
without imposing an invalid half-wrap ordering rule.  The attributed-event
sequence skips its reserved zero sentinel and journal paging/loss detection
remains ordered across `uint32` wrap.
CAN receive-buffer overflow and dropped frames are surfaced in CAN health and
cause running trains to fail closed.

## CAN and terminal reliability

All terminal input/output uses the terminal server.  `Getc` with no input
blocks only its caller; RX/TX notifiers and bounded queues allow the dispatch
ticker, sensor courier, and safety servers to keep running while the prompt is
idle or a large view is printed.  `TryGetc` is a separate nonblocking
observation used by periodic UI clients: it returns a buffered unsigned byte,
an explicit empty result, or an explicit transport/server error.  Repeated
empty polls do not allocate blocking waiter entries and do not disturb
blocking-`Getc` FIFO order.

CAN transmit requests are queued.  The MCP2515 TX buffer is never aborted or
overwritten merely because it is busy.  After TXREQ clears without ABTF,
MLOA, or TXERR, a critical speed, direction, or turnout command remains
pending until the CAN server receives the matching CS3 response with the same
command, DLC, and data and the response bit set.  Only then may the next
command in that batch proceed.  Controller or response timeout, malformed
traffic, software drops, and RXB0/RXB1 hardware overflow update health and
fail motion closed.

Emergency speed-zero batches cancel queued launch/turnout traffic and queued
reverse/resume controls.  The one command already on the wire completes
or reaches its bounded timeout first, preserving the CS3
one-command/one-response rule; the stop is next.  Their completion tokens
remain pending until every stop receives its CS3 confirmation.
Stale-response quarantine cannot poison an identical absolute speed/turnout
retry, while a direction-toggle ambiguity fails closed.  A full retry request
never discards already queued emergency stops.  Temporary queue exhaustion
returns `BUSY` without incrementing transport-failure health, and the
dispatcher retries behind its preparation/launch barrier.  Terminal batch
states use a rotating retention pool so a new batch cannot immediately
overwrite a completion before the dispatcher observes it.  TX and RX notifier
heartbeats plus the sensor-courier heartbeat are runtime launch/motion gates;
ordinary server traffic can continue TX progress even if the TX notifier
stops.  The `sensors` command exposes courier readiness, heartbeat, receive
and failure counters, while `trips` names the exact sensor-health subfault
that caused a fail-stop.

## UI fields

`trips` and `trackview` keep every managed train visible and report:

- train, origin, speed, destination region, planned `anchor_sensor`, user
  endpoint offset and confirmation status (reported separately from the
  observed position);
- launch epoch/state and conflict/wait reason;
- confirmed or estimated current node, next expected sensor, route progress,
  remaining distance, and reservation generation;
- current motion leg and completed/total reversals;
- missing, spurious, duplicate, or journal-loss diagnostics;
- braking/arrival hold state and fail-closed reason;
- for `caldispatch`, provisional velocity/stop prediction, the most recent
  sensor time/distance anchor, predicted/actual speed-zero request ticks,
  matching CAN-confirmation tick, signed request timing error, and trigger;
- CAN failure/drop counters and launch skew.

`actual_request_tick` is the application `Time()` sample taken immediately
before the speed-zero CAN IPC.  `confirmed_tick` is the client-side `Time()`
sample taken after the matching CS3 response returns.  They bound software
request/confirmation timing but are not exact on-wire timestamps; retain the
Wireshark capture for transport-level timing.  If an operator cancels before
the automatic prediction trigger, the row has no automatic trigger and must
not be used as calibration evidence.

The reservation and sensor views are sequential service snapshots and are
labelled as samples; they do not claim cross-server atomicity.

## Calibration data

Version-controlled data is in `calibration/`.  The fallback model is explicitly
labelled `seed`; it is not presented as a physical measurement.  Velocity is
fixed-point tenths of a millimetre per 10 ms tick.  Separate slow/nominal/fast
bounds drive watchdog, display, and braking/earliest-arrival calculations, so
the historical centimetres/second values are never misread as whole
millimetres per tick.  Train-specific lab observations must be appended using
`calibration/track_d_measurement_template.csv` and the code/data updated
together.  The post-stop settling deadline begins only after the matching CS3
speed-zero confirmation and is conservatively derived from slow-bound travel
through the full braking-plus-uncertainty envelope; raw calibration rows
therefore record both stopping distance and observed settle ticks.

The rejected early 2026-07-26 T14/A/speed-20 run is retained in
`calibration/track_d_physical_runs.csv`: only A1 was observed before the seed
model commanded zero near C13.  It is evidence that the seed bounds are not a
T14 calibration, not a replacement velocity constant.  Stage 8 reports this
case as `STOP_UNCONFIRMED` and preserves the remaining route instead of
claiming a physical arrival.

A later supervised TC1 measurement on the same date reached A1, C13, and E7.
Its C13-to-E7 segment measured 39.632 mm/s.  The pickup leading edge finally
stopped 55 mm beyond E7 after the speed-zero request frame was sent
44.757 mm beyond E7,
giving an inferred 10.243 mm command-to-stop distance.  That single run is the
provisional Stage-8 predictor anchor, not accepted calibration: physical
settle time was not measured, no repeat exists, and speeds other than 20 are
extrapolations.  The generic safety envelope remains authoritative until the
required repeated rows are collected.

The 2026-07-27 Stage-8 physical `caldispatch 14 A 20 d7` run exposed a
separate envelope-integration error. CAN confirmed the positive-speed
command, then the sensor service correctly attributed A1 at about 859 ticks
and C13 at about 1769 ticks after launch. The dispatcher nevertheless charged
both as spurious and failed closed near C13 because it had reused the narrow
provisional point-prediction band as a certified maximum-speed sensor bound.
Stage 9 keeps those concerns separate: the generic conservative fast envelope
validates whether an ordered sensor occurrence is physically possible, while
the provisional model is confined to supervised timing deadlines—the point
stop-command prediction and its fail-closed watchdog. A captured-timing
regression replays both
observations, requires zero sensor errors, re-anchors progress at C13 (973 of
2129 mm), preserves the reservation, and predicts from the remaining 1156 mm
toward E7 and D7.

The subsequent Stage-9 run in `6.pcapng` reached A1, C13, E7, and physical
sensor D7 with no route-order error.  It then requested speed zero about
2.906 ms after the physical D7 report and stopped near that sensor.  Physical
measurement established that the operator-labelled user endpoint D7 is
downstream of the sensor, so the run was semantically short and is not
accepted calibration.  Stage 10 separated the concepts, but its +180 mm run
still stopped 100 mm before the labelled endpoint.  Stage 11 used a directed
+280 mm endpoint and updated point velocity from every accepted ordered
sensor interval at every command speed 1..120.  Its predicted speed-zero
request differed from the actual request by only one 10 ms tick, but the
settled speed-20 train remained 68 mm before the labelled endpoint.  A later
physical speed-100 run using the same +280 mm base stopped exactly at the
labelled endpoint.  Stage 13 therefore kept the physical geometry fixed at
+280 mm and added a first two-point correction.  Subsequent physical tests of
that version found the settled train 93 mm short at speeds 20/40/60/80,
4 mm beyond at speed 100, and 240 mm beyond at speed 120.  Stage 14 folded
those residuals into the six signed correction anchors
+161/+145/+127/+110/-4/-240 mm and piecewise-interpolates every integer speed
1..120.  A subsequent Stage-14 retest found speeds 20 through 100 acceptable,
but speed 120 still stopped 180 mm beyond the labelled endpoint.  Stage 15
therefore leaves all lower anchors unchanged and changes only the speed-120
anchor to -420 mm.  Two Stage-15 repetitions then both stopped 40 mm beyond
the labelled endpoint.  Stage 16 keeps every anchor through speed 100 and
changes only speed 120 to -460 mm.  Speeds 101..120 interpolate to the earlier high-speed
request; when that request lies before D7 the route uses E7 as its physical
anchor without changing the user destination or scalar endpoint.  Route cost
and point deadlines use the effective speed-specific
endpoint, while collision protection continues to use its independent
conservative braking envelope.  Because this endpoint crosses a
turnout boundary,
a later `CURRENT` command keeps the complete braking/turnout reservation but
derives its position ambiguity only from the travelled corridor between the
last trusted sensor and the virtual endpoint.  Adjacent paired-turnout safety
resources therefore remain protected without being misclassified as possible
train locations.
Only this directed D7 base and its six single-run correction anchors have
physical evidence; the anchors still require repeated runs, and interpolated
speeds and the other directed sides remain clearly marked provisional.

## Physical Track D verification

Before a graded or unattended run:

1. build and upload the TC2 image using the course AArch64 toolchain;
2. verify A–F placement/orientation and all d1–d8 labels against the physical
   layout;
3. calibrate each train/speed/direction used in the demo and record the raw
   rows under `calibration/`, including the lowest speed that repeatedly moves
   without stalling, measured stopping distance, and post-confirmation settle
   ticks;
4. run one train at low speed through both sides of every destination and each
   required reversal;
5. run two conflicting trips and verify the second remains stopped;
6. run 3-, 4-, and 6-job epochs with disjoint and conflicting subsets;
7. inject one intermediate missing report and one non-target spurious report
   separately and verify the trip succeeds conservatively; inject a second
   error and verify fail-stop;
8. verify no turnout changes beneath a train and inspect CAN
   drop/overflow/timeout counters;
9. use the emergency stop whenever the physical result differs from the model.

Software tests prove the state-machine and data-structure invariants; only
these physical checks validate train-specific stopping and real layout
behavior.

## Stage 33 optimized renderer bound

The sensor-flash pass revalidates every directed sensor's non-negative
column, positive width, and complete `[column, column + width)` interval
against the 132-column render frame immediately before writing styles.
Invalid catalog geometry is skipped without a partial write.  The host
renderer regression now compiles with `-O3`, matching the optimization level
that exposed the AArch64 `stringop-overflow` diagnostic.

## Stage 34 target-compiler closure

The directed-sensor catalog stores columns and widths as unsigned bytes.
Stage 34 removes the invalid signed-negative comparison rejected by the course
AArch64 compiler under `-O3 -Werror`, promotes both values to `size_t`, and
checks the complete interval before indexing the style frame.  The runtime,
post-link gate, build-isolation test, and documentation now share the exact
Stage 34 marker.  Host `-O3` and sanitizer tests cover the same write site;
the course AArch64 build remains the final target-toolchain check.
